      "sourceLink": null,
      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "The system the devs made is only able to prevent private avatars from being loaded using a non-owner account.\nThe main issue with preventing content ripping is the simple undeniable fact that, in order for you to actually use the content (see the avatar and enter a world), you have to download all the required files for using/rendering it.\nEven if you were to implement encryption this still can be just as easy bypassed due to the same reason as prior stated, that and using encryption would reduce performance with no actual security being added.\nCurrently the only effective way to prevent content ripping is by using game streaming services, essentially that what you see is a pre-rendered video/stream and no content/assets had to be downloaded to see this on your screen.\nBut VRC is not a streamed service because the rendering power required to maintain this for all the users would be at bare minimum a GeForce 1060.\nThis would mean a minimum cost of 400$ multiplied by number of users, so for example 10000*400 = 4 million.\nThis is the cost just to get ass grade rendering comparable to Quest users, and if someone wants better performance there is no way for them to achieve this.\nOn the bright side, what people cannot rip is the skills and effort required to make these worlds & avatars, the original unity project used to make these is not uploaded in it's entierty, meaning that if some noob that cannot make a avatar to even save he's own life is cocky enough to claim that he made the content that he ripped, the original maker can easy make a complete workover in a fraction of the time.\nA reverse compiler does not restore the code developers documentation, meaning ripped content is difficult to modify depending on how complex the content is.",
      "viewerReactions": null
    },
